The copy of this file in the npm package also holds the notice of every package bundled into it, which its build adds.

appdyno's Android agent (Stream*.kt, DisplayMirror.kt, Animations.kt) makes its display and encoder as the platform
behaves, which was read in the source of scrcpy (https://github.com/Genymobile/scrcpy, Apache License 2.0): which
hidden calls exist on which release, the sizes an encoder refuses, and a separate shell process that puts a setting
back when the agent dies. No code of it is here.

appdyno's iPhone stream (packages/driver-apple/src/phone-stream) speaks to services a phone offers its Mac, as public
notes describe them: the names of those services, the shape of their requests, the fields of a stream's offer, and what
a phone expects back on RTCP and takes as input were read in the source of idevice
(https://github.com/jkcoxson/idevice), whose authors worked them out. What was run on a phone here confirmed them. No
code of it is here. That folder can be deleted as a whole: all else still builds, and an iPhone keeps its runner.

appdyno's Android push by FCM's own message (packages/driver-android/src/fcm-local.ts) sends the broadcast Google Play
services hands an app, as the Firebase Android SDK reads it, which was read in its source
(https://github.com/firebase/firebase-android-sdk, Apache License 2.0): the receiver and the action, the names of the
extras, and which of them make a notification. No code of it is here. The push suite's own app,
android/push-fixture, is built with that SDK's Firebase Messaging, which ships in no package of appdyno.

The Mac app carries Perfetto's trace processor (trace_processor_shell, https://github.com/google/perfetto, Apache
License 2.0), which reads Android's traces, as Google's release builds it and unchanged but for the app's signature. Its
license and notices are in licenses/perfetto.txt, as that release gives them. It also carries Node.js
(https://nodejs.org), as its release builds it, with its symbols stripped and the app's signature added. Its license,
with those of what Node.js itself includes, is in licenses/node.txt.

